.TH Objectspace-TERMINAL 1 "@DATE@" "Objectspace @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
osd-terminal \- Objectspace Terminal, a program on a terminal in an Objectspace window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B osd-terminal
.RB [ \-\-title
.IR TITLE ]
.RB [ \-\-hold ]
.RB [ \-\-font
.IR SPEC ]
.RB [ \-\-cwd
.IR FOLDER ]
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR COLS x ROWS ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR COMMAND " [" ARG ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B osd-terminal
runs a program (the user's shell if none is given) on a pseudo-terminal in
an Objectspace window, as OS/2's command window did. Objectspace starts it for text-mode
programs (the System's Programs page) and for
.BR "Command prompt here" .
It draws in the scheme in use and the System's Terminal font; colours (true
colour too), full-screen programs, the mouse, wide characters and input
methods work. One window is one process: the window lives as long as the
program, and goes with a work area's group.
.PP
Shift+Page Up and Shift+Page Down scroll back; Ctrl+Shift+C and
Ctrl+Shift+V copy and paste; objects dropped on the window are typed in as
their paths.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-title " TITLE"
The window's title (a program may set its own).
.TP
.B \-\-hold
When the program ends, the window stays with its status until Enter.
.TP
.BI \-\-font " SPEC"
A font as the Font Palette names it (for example
.BR 12.Monospace );
by default the System's Terminal font, else
.BR 11.Monospace .
.TP
.BI \-\-cwd " FOLDER"
Where the program starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " COLS" x ROWS
The window's size in characters (80x24).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B TERM
is set to
.B xterm-256color
and
.B COLORTERM
to
.B truecolor
for the program.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osd (1),
.BR osd-shell (1)
